<h1>{{ unit }}</h1>
<div class="stats">
  {% for value, name in stats %}
  <div class="stat"><b>{{ value }}</b><span>{{ name }}</span></div>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
<details class="guide">
  <summary>How to read this report</summary>
  <dl>
    <dt>Hit</dt>
    <dd>
      A position where the unit fires strongly: an image patch for a vision unit, a token for a
      text unit. Hits are the positive activations past the elbow of the sorted curve (section 1).
    </dd>
    <dt>Hit vector</dt>
    <dd>
      The exact contribution of every upstream channel to one hit: upstream activation times the
      unit's weight for that channel. It sums to the unit's pre-activation minus its bias. Drawn as
      a grid of channels: green pushes the unit up, red pushes it down.
    </dd>
    <dt>Distance</dt>
    <dd>
      Weighted Jaccard between hit vectors, positive and negative parts compared separately. 0 means
      the same channels drove both hits in the same amounts, 1 means no channel in common.
    </dd>
    <dt>Forest and clusters</dt>
    <dd>
      Each hit links to its nearest denser hit. Hits that are both dense and far from anything
      denser become roots, and every other hit climbs to one root. Each root's tree is a cluster
      (section 3).
    </dd>
    <dt>Probability and entropy</dt>
    <dd>
      Each hit gets a probability for every cluster from its graph distance to that cluster's peak.
      A hit's cluster is its most probable one. Entropy in bits says how split it is; under 0.5 is
      confident.
    </dd>
    <dt>Map</dt>
    <dd>
      Hits laid out by a spring layout on their real distances, with an extra pull toward the peaks
      of the clusters they likely belong to. Nothing is projected: a hit between two clusters sits
      between their peaks.
    </dd>
    <dt>DeepLift</dt>
    <dd>
      Which input pixels drove this hit, against a black baseline. Yellow raises the unit, blue
      lowers it. The box marks the hit's position.
    </dd>
    <dt>Group clusters</dt>
    <dd>
      Hits that need the same set of clusters to cover 85% of their probability: the borders
      between clusters.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</details>
